  "intuition_before": "**Before the numbers.** Two-bar y is the honest next question after Round 3: if 1-bar\nnext-close-vs-typical is just this close vs this typical (rule 61.90%, model \u22120.15 pp),\nthen looking at bar t+2 either (1) the known offset still leaks into y because FX 5m\ncloses stay near this close, so A is a weaker copy of R (~55\u201358%), or (2) two bars is\nenough that R dies and we finally measure leftover skill. CEO G1 said leftover 5m\ndirection on this X is a coin; A-residual vs 50% is that test on a longer horizon.\nB is two-bar close-to-close. One-bar close-to-close was already ~50.9%. Belief: B is\nstill a coin, R is the wrong baseline for B, and close-to-close persistence\n(sign(close_t \u2212 close_{t\u22121})) is the fair sibling \u2014 also near 50%. The 180 will still\nagree with R on A because X is a close-vs-mean machine. No new columns can change that.\n",
  "intuition_after": "A: 180 is 58.27% vs rule 58.41% (-0.14 pp). Agree-with-rule 91.66%; disagree acc 49.14%. Residual (y!=R) n=27,228 acc 9.85% vs 50% (-40.15 pp). R still owns A at two bars \u2014 the known offset survives a 10-minute wait, just weaker (58.4% vs Round 3 61.9%). Belief (1) held. Belief (2) did not: two bars did not kill R. A-residual is far below 50% because the 180 still copies R, so it is systematically wrong on R's misses. A coin would be 50%. CEO G1 stands: no leftover 5m direction skill. B: 180 is 51.12% vs rule 49.66% (+1.46 pp), agree-with-rule 30.34%. One-bar close-to-close was ~50.9%. B is still a coin. Two-bar close-to-close did not create a forecast. Belief on B held. R is the wrong baseline for B (agrees 30%). B persist sign(close_t - close_t-1) = 49.69%. Placebos A 49.74% / 50.15%; B 50.09% / 49.85%. Do not add columns.",
  "round3_ref": {
    "A_1bar_logistic_180": 0.6175,
    "A_1bar_rule": 0.619,
    "B_1bar_close_to_close": 0.509
  }
